From: OpenAI-assisted development
Subject: [PATCH] rtw88: honor userspace regulatory domain on blank-efuse cards

The DIR-842 R1's RTL8822BE has a blank efuse, including country_code=ff:ff.
The old rtw88 regulatory path maps that to country 00 and then ignores
NL80211_REGDOM_SET_BY_USER notifications. As a result, a valid userspace
country such as BR cannot fully replace the driver's worldwide channel
flags; UNII-3 channels can remain NO-IR even when the regulatory database
permits AP operation.

Later upstream rtw88 versions distinguish programmed-country cards from
worldwide-roaming cards and allow userspace regulatory settings for the
latter. Backport the minimum safe part of that behavior here:

- preserve the existing "ignore user request" behavior when efuse contains
  a programmed country;
- accept the user request when both efuse country bytes are 0xff;
- re-run the existing beacon/channel flag reconciliation using the active
  regulatory rules.

This does not raise TX power beyond cfg80211/regdb limits and does not
hard-code BR. It only lets the configured country take effect on hardware
whose efuse contains no country.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/regd.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/regd.c
@@ -378,7 +378,21 @@ static int rtw_regd_notifier_apply(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev,
 				   struct wiphy *wiphy,
 				   struct regulatory_request *request)
 {
-	if (request->initiator == NL80211_REGDOM_SET_BY_USER)
-		return 0;
+	/*
+	 * A programmed efuse country remains authoritative, preserving the
+	 * behavior of this old rtw88 backport.  Blank-efuse devices, however,
+	 * are worldwide-roaming hardware: userspace must be allowed to select
+	 * the actual operating country.
+	 *
+	 * The DIR-842 R1 RTL8822BE is exactly this case (country_code ff:ff).
+	 * Ignoring NL80211_REGDOM_SET_BY_USER here leaves the initial "00"
+	 * world-domain NO-IR flags stuck on channels that the selected regdb
+	 * country would otherwise permit for AP operation.
+	 */
+	if (request->initiator == NL80211_REGDOM_SET_BY_USER &&
+	    rtwdev->efuse.country_code[0] != 0xff &&
+	    rtwdev->efuse.country_code[1] != 0xff)
+		return 0;
+
 	rtwdev->regd = rtw_regd_find_reg_by_name(request->alpha2);
 	rtw_regd_apply_world_flags(wiphy, request->initiator);
